HST Office: inline objects (MathType formulas above all) ignored the run's <w:position>.

Word puts an inline object's bottom on the baseline and then raises or lowers the whole run by
w:position (half-points). MathType writes that value on every inline formula run (Equation.DSMT4
OLE objects, usually -2 pt to -18 pt) so the formula's own baseline meets the text baseline.
Writer import deferred w:position until the run's text is appended, which never happens for a
run that only holds an object, so the value was dropped: every formula sat raised by that amount,
lines with formulas grew taller and a 6-page worksheet became 7 pages.

Import (writerfilter, shared by .docx and .rtf): an as-character OLE object, DrawingML picture or
VML shape in a run with w:position gets VertOrient NONE at -height - position * 2540 / 144
(the object's top relative to the baseline, downwards).
Export: an as-character object with VertOrient NONE writes the run's w:position (.docx) or
sprmCHpsPos (.doc) as -(position + height) in half-points; Math formulas keep their own
alignment. The .docx run never gets two w:position elements.

Test: tests/runtime/check-docx-word-interop.py (docx_inline_object_position.py).

